.po 1i
.TH ECHO 1C UCB
.V 08/15/84 16:48:00
.\" @[$]echo.1C	1.5  08/15/84 16:48:00 - Zilog Inc
.ad
.fi
.SH NAME
echo \- echo (print) arguments to the standard output (terminal) 
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B echo
[ -n ] [ \fIarg\fR ] ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Echo\^ 
is both an internal shell command, and an external program,
it writes its arguments separated by blanks and terminated by
a new-line on the standard output.
It also
understands C-like escape conventions;
beware of conflicts with the shell's use of \fB\e\fP:
.PP
.RS
.PD 0
.TP
.B \eb
backspace
.TP
.B \ec
print line without new-line
.TP
.B \ef
form-feed
.TP
.B \en
new-line
.TP
.B \er
carriage return
.TP
.B \et
tab
.TP
.B \e\e
backslash
.TP
.BI \e n\^
the 8-bit character whose \s-1ASCII\s0 code is
the 1-, 2- or 3-digit octal number
.IR n ,
which must start with a zero.
.RE
.PD
.PP
.B Echo\^
is useful for producing diagnostics in command files
and for sending known data into a pipe.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B -n 
No newline is added to the output.
.SH EXAMPLE
.RS
     % \fBecho\fR this is using the echo command
     this is using the echo command
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
csh(1), echo(1), echo2(1).
.br
\fIThe C Shell\fR in the ZEUS Utilities Manual

